# SNHU CS 255

## Briefly summarize the DriverPass project. Who was the client? What type of system did they want you to design? What did you do particularly well?
The DriverPass project involved designing a comprehesnive web-based management system for a new driving school to replace manual, paper-based processes. The clients were Liam and Ian, the owner and IT Officer, respectively.
The system designed was a blended model (object and process) that handled student registration, secure payment processing, lesson scheduling for a fleet of cars, and progress tracking for DMV test prepration.
I believe I succeeded in creating a highly detailed class diagram that captured the specific client requests from the transcript.

## If you could choose one part of your work on these documents to revise, what would you pick? How would you improve it?
If I could go back and revise something I would improve system limitations section of the design document. I would like to expand on the vehicle management logic as I feel like I left it relatively undetailed. I also feel like I could do a lot better with the UML diagrams now, being more familiar with using Lucidchart.

## How did you interpret the user’s needs and implement them into your system design? Why is it so important to consider the user’s needs when designing?
I interpreted the user's needs by analyzing the interview transcript for pain points as well as non-negotiable requirements. When designing a system, it is important to recognize both what you are trying to do and what you are trying to avoid, and negotiate between both of them.
It's important to consider the user's needs when designing because no matter how well you design a program (in your opinion) it is worthless if nobody enjoys using it.

## How do you approach designing software? What techniques or strategies would you use in the future to analyze and design a system?
I approached designing software with the SDLC in mind, focusing on transitioning from business requirements to technical models. The UML modeling is a universal language to bridge the gap between client wants and what the developer needs to do. This is similar to me when finding the requirements, needing to know what to avoid and what to build towards.
In the future, I will use Iterative Prototyping and CASE tools like Lucidchart more extensively. These strategies allow for rapid visualization of complex logic (like my Sequence Diagrams) and make it easier to get early feedback from stakeholders before any code is actually written and technical debt is incurred.
